Case report
Stiff-person syndrome associated with oral isotretinoin treatment
E. Chronia,*, Th. Sakkisb, S. Georgioub, A. Monastirlib, E. Pasmatzib, C. Paschalisa, D. Tsambaosb
aDepartment of Neurology, School of Medicine, University of Patras, P.O. Box 1045, Rio, Patras 26500, Greece
bDepartment of Dermatology, School of Medicine, University of Patras, P.O. Box 1413, Rio, Patras 26500, Greece
Received 30 November 2001; received in revised form 11 March 2002; accepted 20 May 2002
Abstract
We describe a patient with severe nodulocystic acne who developed disabling muscle stiffness and painful superimposed spasms of the
neck, back and upper limbs 10 days after the onset of oral isotretinoin treatment. The muscle hyperactivity condition, which revealed the
clinical and electromyographic features of the stiff-person syndrome, gradually resolved 2 weeks after drug withdrawal. q 2002 Elsevier
Science B.V. All rights reserved.
Keywords: Retinoids; Muscle; Nervous system
1. Introduction
Oral isotretinoin, representative of the ﬁrst retinoid
generation, is presently regarded as the drug of choice for
the systemic treatment of severe forms of acne and rosacea
[1]. Amongst the side effects of this compound, myalgia,
arthralgia, muscle weakness and even true myopathy have
been described [2]. We report a case of disabling general-
ized muscle stiffness, similar to stiff-person syndrome
(SPS), associated with oral isotretinoin administration.
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">An 18-year-old male patient with severe nodulocystic
acne unresponsive to topical remedies and systemic antibio-
tics was given oral isotretinoin (1 mg/kg/day) by a practi-
cing dermatologist subsequent to unremarkable routine
laboratory investigations. Ten days later, he was admitted
to the Department of Dermatology at the Patras University
Hospital complaining of aching and stiffness of his
shoulders, neck, back and thighs. There was no family
history of neuromuscular disease or history of systemic
illness, vigorous physical activity or concomitant intake of
other drugs. Neurological examination showed normal
mental status and cranial nerves. On the up-right position,
the neck was slightly ﬂexed and drawn down into the
shoulder girdle, the arms ﬂexed 308at the elbow, the lumbar
curve exaggerated (hyperlordosis) and the hips as well as
knees ﬂexed 30 8. The patient’s volitional activity was
signiﬁcantly reduced and he moved with great difﬁculty,
like a ‘wooden soldier’ or a ‘robot’. There was marked,
stable resistance to passive movements throughout the
entire range of motion of the neck, shoulders, elbows, hips
and knees. The muscle strength and sensory perception were
normal, the tendon reﬂexes were brisk bilaterally and the
plantar responses ﬂexor. Over the next 2 days the muscle
stiffness continued to worsen and was punctuated by painful
spasms elicited by various startling stimuli such as a touch
on the shoulder or the telephone ringing. The patient was
unable to get out of the bed and walk unaided.
